<div class="main-text" style="text-align: justify;">
    <p><b><i><h1>Blueberry</h1></b><br>

    Blueberries are a popular fruit in the UK, known for their high nutritional value and adaptability to different growing conditions. 
    They thrive best in <strong>southern and central regions</strong>, where the climate is milder, but with proper care, they can also be grown in 
    <strong>northern areas</strong> and <strong>Scotland</strong>. In colder regions, growing blueberries in <strong>containers</strong> or <strong>greenhouses</strong> can help protect them 
    from harsh weather.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Best Growing Conditions</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Climate:</strong> Blueberries grow well in temperate climates with cool winters and mild summers.<br>
    - <strong>Soil:</strong> They require acidic soil (pH between 4.5 and 5.5). If soil pH is too high, consider growing them in raised beds or pots with ericaceous compost.<br>
    - <strong>Sunlight:</strong> Full sun is ideal, but they can tolerate partial shade.<br>
    - <strong>Watering:</strong> They need consistent moisture, especially during fruiting. Rainwater is preferred, as tap water can raise soil pH.<br>
    - <strong>Spacing:</strong> Space bushes around 1.5m apart to allow good air circulation and growth.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Common Blueberry Diseases</h2></b><br>
    The current database does not contain any records of blueberry-related diseases.<br><br>

    <b><h2>Tips for Growing Blueberries in the UK</h2></b><br>
    - <strong>Choose the right variety:</strong> Some of the best varieties for the UK include ‘Bluecrop’, ‘Duke’, and ‘Patriot’.<br>
    - <strong>Mulching:</strong> Apply organic mulch (e.g., pine bark or wood chips) to retain moisture and maintain soil acidity.<br>
    - <strong>Pruning:</strong> Remove old and weak branches in winter to encourage new growth and better fruit production.<br>
    - <strong>Winter Protection:</strong> While hardy, blueberries may benefit from fleece covering in very harsh winters, especially in Scotland.<br>
    - <strong>Bird Protection:</strong> Birds love blueberries! Use netting to protect ripening fruit.<br><br>

    With the right care, blueberries can be a productive and rewarding crop in UK gardens, offering delicious fruit year after year.
    </i></p>
